div3.p:
8. The pure in heart - The sanctified: they who love God
with all their hearts. They shall see God - In all things here;
hereafter in glory.

5. He would have put him to death - ln his fit of passion;
but he was then restrained by fear of the multitude; and afterward by
the reverence he bore him.

28. Moreover the Son of man is Lord even of the Sabbath -
Being the supreme Lawgiver, he hath power to dispense with his own laws;
and with this in particular.

30. Passing through the midst of them - Perhaps invisibly;
or perhaps they were overawed; so that though they saw, they could not
touch him.

46. He wrote of me - Everywhere; in all his writings;
particularly Deut. xviii, 15,
18.

25. He that loveth his life - More than the will of God;
shall lose it eternally: and he that hateth his life - In comparison of
the will of God, shall preserve it. Matt. x, 39.

31. But these things are written that ye may believe - That
ye may be confirmed in believing. Faith cometh sometimes by reading;
though ordinarily by hearing.

9. Purifying - This word is repeated from chap. x, 15;
their hearts - The heart is the proper seat of purity; by faith -
Without concerning themselves with the Mosaic law.

23. He commanded the centurion to let him have liberty - To
be only a prisoner at large. Hereby the Gospel was spread more and more;
not to the satisfaction of the Jews. But they could not hinder it.

4. Thus ye also - Are now as free from the Mosaic law as an
husband is, when his wife is dead. By the body of Christ - Offered up;
that is, by the merits of his death, that law expiring with him.

2. Because such a doctrine would not only
have had no tendency to convince, but would have evidently tended to
harden, the Jews;

23. Ye are bought with a price - Ye belong to God;
therefore, where it can be avoided, do not become the bondslaves of men
- Which may expose you to many temptations.

20. Therefore - That is, in consequence of those schisms.
It is not eating the Lord's supper - That solemn memorial of his death;
but quite another thing.

1. Stand fast therefore in the liberty - From the
ceremonial law. Wherewith Christ hath made us - And all believers, free;
and be not entangled again with the yoke of legal bondage.

3. The mystery - Of salvation by Christ alone, and that
both to Jews and gentiles. As I wrote before - Namely, chap. i, 9, 10;
the very words of which passage he here repeats.

1. What was proposed, chap. i, 5, 6, is now more largely
treated of: concerning Paul and his fellowlabourers, ver. 1-12;
concerning the Thessalonians, ver. 13-16.

4. Whosoever committeth sin - Thereby transgresseth the
holy, just, and good law of God, and so sets his authority at nought;
for this is implied in the very nature of sin.

div4.p:
29, 30. We have here the third part of the sixth day's
work, which was not any new creation, but a gracious provision of food
for all flesh, Psalm 1xxxvi, 25.-Here is,

5. He must pitch it within and without:
without, to shed off the rain, and to prevent the water from soaking in;
within, to take away the ill smell of the beasts when kept close.

17. And behold, I, even I, do bring a flood of waters
upon the earth - I that am infinite in power, and therefore can do it;
infinite in justice, and therefore will do it.

1. And when Abram was ninety nine years old - Full
thirteen years after the birth of Ishmael. So long the promise of Isaac
was deferred;

III. Abimelech's discourse with Abraham;
wherein he chides him for the cheat he had put upon him, ver. 8, 9, 10.
and Abraham excuses it as well as he can, ver. 11, 12, 13.

6. Thou art a prince of God among us - So the word is;
not only great, but good. He called himself a stranger and a sojourner,
they call him a great prince.

13. Moses sat to judge the people - To answer enquiries;
to acquaint them with the will of God in doubtful cases, and to explain
the laws of God that were already given.

8. A sanctuary - A place of public and solemn worship;
that I may dwell among them. Not by my essence, which is everywhere; but
by my grace and glorious operations.

2. Mercy entailed upon thousands of
generations, even to those upon whom the ends of the world are come;
nay, the line of it is drawn parallel with that of eternity itself.

46. He shall dwell alone - Partly for his humiliation;
partly to prevent the infection of others; and partly to shew the danger
of converse with spiritual lepers, or notorious sinners.

29. Do not prostitute - As the Gentiles frequently did
for the honour of some of their idols, to whom women were consecrated,
and publickly prostituted.

13. Before the Lord - In thy private addresses to God;
for this is to be said presently upon the distribution of these tithes,
which was not done at Jerusalem, but in their own private gates or
dwellings. And this is to be spoken before the Lord, that is, solemnly,
seriously, and in a religious manner, with due respect to God's
presence, and will, and glory.

2. To Ai - They were not to go into the city of Ai, but
into the country belonging to it, to understand the state of the place;
and the people.

9. The stranger - Not only proselytes, but others also;
because this was a matter of common right, that a distinction might be
made between casual man-slayers, and wilful murderers.

16. Bread - That is, meat, as bread is commonly taken in
scripture. To the Lord - Not unto a man, as thou apprehendest me to be;
but unto the Lord, as thou wilt by and by perceive me to be.

5. Three days - As long as the law required, Exod. xix, 15. And so long David, and his men hid, it seems, hid
themselves for fear of Saul, whereby they were kept both from women: and
from food convenient for them. Vessels - That is, Either,

25. A rock - That is, into a cave which was in the rock;
where at first he might think to hide himself, but upon farther
consideration he removed from thence upon Saul's approach.

24. A band - Of soldiers, who fled upon that defeat, 2 Sam. x, 18, and others who readily joined them, and lived by robbery;
as many Arabians did. Damascus - And took it, whilst Solomon was
wallowing in luxury.

37. Died - Finding too late the truth of Micaiah's words;
and Zedekiah's horns of iron, pushing not the Syrians, but himself, into
destruction.

1. To their gross idolatries, and other
abominable practices, which they were ashamed to own before others;
or,

10. Made him the chief - Not in inheriting the estate;
(this was forbidden by the law) but in this service, for which he was
better qualified than his elder brother.

2. But not, &c. - He was not an enemy to religion,
but a cool and indifferent friend. He was not a man of serious piety;
for his heart was not whole with God.

2. The Buzite - Of the posterity of Buz, Nahor's son, Gen. xxii,
21. Ram - Or, of Aram; for Ram and Aram are used promiscuously;
compare

9. He said - Not in express terms, but by consequence;
because he said that good men were no less, nay, sometimes more
miserable here than the wicked.

8. Fret not - Either against the sinner for his success;
or against God. Do evil - If grief arise in thee, take care that it do
not transport thee to sin.

10. The sea - Of remote countries, to which they used to
go from Canaan by sea; which are frequently called isles in scripture;
the kings that rule by sea or by land.

12. Cut off - As men do their grapes in time of vintage;
so the Hebrew verb implies. The spirit - Their breath and life, as he
did in the Assyrian army.

11. Thoughts - Yes, he knoweth all things, even the most
secret things, as the thoughts of men; and in particular your thoughts;
much more your practices.

8. Them - The intercessors before mentioned. Forgavest -
The people for whom they prayed, so far as not to inflict that total
destruction upon them which they deserved;

32. Exalt him - In public assemblies, and before all
persons, as they have opportunity. Elders - The magistrates or rulers;
let them not be ashamed nor afraid to speak of God's wonderful works,
before the greatest of men.

An exhortation to cleave to wisdom, as a preservative
from strange women, ver. 1-5. The snares they lay for unwary young men,
ver. 6-23. A serious caution against coming near them, ver. 24-37.

1. I saw - In a vision. The Lord - The Divine Majesty as
he subsisteth in three persons. His train - His royal and judicial robe;
for he is represented as a judge.

11. Spake - With a vehement and more than ordinary
inspiration. In the way - Of the generality of the people of Judah;
whose eminent danger and calamity he foretells.

21. These - These things, the deep ignorance and
stupidity of idolaters. Forgotten - I will not forget nor forsake thee;
therefore thou shalt have no need of idols.

18. This whole land - All its inhabitants in general;
intimating, that though men of all degrees should set themselves against
him, yet God would support him against their all, and would carry him
thro' his work, tho' his troubles would not be only great, but long;
passing thro' several king's reigns.

5. This night - They would lose neither day nor night;
which shews that, they were extraordinarily stirred up by God in this
expedition.

16. Was not this - They only truly know God who obey him;
men vainly pretend to piety who are defective in justice and
charity.

15. All their gates - Both of cities, of palaces, and of
private houses. Wrapt up - And hath been carefully kept in the scabbard,
that it might not be blunted.

6. And blood - Thy guilt, and my just revenge of innocent
blood. Hast not hated - Thou hast loved, rather than hated, blood-shed;
therefore vengeance for it follows thee.

4. Seleucus had Babylon and Syria. All these were
variously situated; to the east, Babylon and Syria; to the south, Egypt;
to the north, Asia the less; to the west, Greece.
